Policy "Parameterized SQL only":
SQL must be built with parameters or an ORM, never by concatenating or formatting strings with values that come from outside the program. Table and column names taken from constants in the code are fine.

<untrusted_code>
File: src/AccountRepo.java
Lines: 17-17
Rule message: Possible violation of the policy "Parameterized SQL only".

Code (flagged lines marked >>):
       1 | import java.sql.Connection;
       2 | import java.sql.PreparedStatement;
       3 | import java.sql.ResultSet;
       4 | import java.sql.SQLException;
       5 | import java.sql.Statement;
       6 | 
       7 | public class AccountRepo {
       8 |     private static final String TABLE_NAME = "accounts";
       9 |     private final Connection conn;
      10 | 
      11 |     public AccountRepo(Connection conn) {
      12 |         this.conn = conn;
      13 |     }
      14 | 
      15 |     public ResultSet findByOwner(String ownerName) throws SQLException {
      16 |         Statement stmt = conn.createStatement();
>>    17 |         return stmt.executeQuery("SELECT * FROM accounts WHERE owner = '" + ownerName + "'");
      18 |     }
      19 | 
      20 |     public ResultSet findById(long id) throws SQLException {
      21 |         PreparedStatement ps = conn.prepareStatement("SELECT * FROM " + TABLE_NAME + " WHERE id = ?");
      22 |         ps.setLong(1, id);
      23 |         return ps.executeQuery();
      24 |     }
      25 | }
</untrusted_code>

Why the reviewer kept it: Line 17 builds a SQL query by concatenating the method parameter ownerName, which comes from callers outside the program, directly into the statement text and runs it with executeQuery on a plain Statement. This is the SQL injection pattern the policy forbids; it should use a PreparedStatement with a ? placeholder.

